the divisive nature of american politics  has seen the threat against politicians  rise four-fold in the last year  even at the u.s capitol one of the most  protected buildings in the world  capitol police couldn't protect them  an angry mob storming congress amid  claims of a stolen election  five people died in the attack and  elected officials were left fearing for  their lives  in the weeks that followed a huge  military and security presence  surrounded capitol hill  and many politicians stepped up their  own personal security  some even buying body armor or carrying  a firearm retired major general linda  singh was one of those tasked with  reviewing their security the key thing  is how can we ensure their safety but  yet allow them to do what they're hired  to do right i mean they're elected to be  out in the public and so how do we  get that fine balance and how have you  struck the balance  well i think it's challenging i think  it's challenging because every member as  you can imagine has their own view of  security and so one of the things that  you know you really have to do is to get  personal with that individual to say  okay how are we going to work this what  works for you may not work for the next  person  congressional spending on private  security has surged among members of  both parties since january the sixth  attack  republican liz cheney spent 58 thousand  dollars on protective measures in the  first three months of this year after  publicly denouncing donald trump for  inciting the violence  fellow republican senator mitt romney  spent more than 43 thousand dollars on  security after voting to impeach the  former president  u.s politicians don't usually meet  members of the public without an  appointment and when they hold town  halls which is about as close as you get  to a constituency surgery over here  they're allowed to expense security  detail and body armor but if they want  security at home or bodyguards for when  they're out and about they have to pay  for it themselves the most recent  targeted attacks on individual members  of congress include democrat gabrielle  giffords who was shot at point-blank  range during a constituents meeting  outside a supermarket in arizona ten  years ago she survived  in 2015 senator clementa pinckney was  killed in a mass shooting at a historic  black church in south carolina in what  was thought to be a hate crime political  spending on security here is roughly  three times what it was two years ago  members of congress feeling increasingly  threatened by the very public they're  elected to represent  sally lockwood sky news washington
 

